---
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const description = "Understand the practical difference between an electronic visa (eVisa) and an electronic travel authorisation (ETA), including how each affects passport mobility scores.";
const structuredData = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Article",
    headline: "eVisa vs ETA: what is the difference?",
    description,
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    mainEntity: [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        name: "Is an ETA the same as an eVisa?",
        acceptedAnswer: {
          "@type": "Answer",
          text: "No. An ETA is usually advance permission to travel for a traveller who is otherwise visa-exempt. An eVisa is a visa obtained through an electronic application process.",
        },
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        name: "Does an ETA or eVisa guarantee entry?",
        acceptedAnswer: {
          "@type": "Answer",
          text: "No. Approval normally permits travel or supplies the required visa, but border authorities still make the final entry decision.",
        },
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        name: "Why does MultiPass Rank count ETA access but not eVisa access?",
        acceptedAnswer: {
          "@type": "Answer",
          text: "The mobility score measures destinations accessible without obtaining a visa before departure. ETA travel is generally visa-exempt, while an eVisa remains a visa that requires prior approval.",
        },
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="eVisa vs ETA: What Is the Difference? | MultiPass Rank"
  description={description}
  canonical="/evisa-vs-eta"
  markdownHref="/evisa-vs-eta.md"
  structuredData={structuredData}
>
  <article class="prose term-guide">
    <span class="eyebrow">Travel access, plainly explained</span>
    <h1>eVisa vs ETA: what is the difference?</h1>
    <p class="term-guide__lead">
      Both are requested online before a trip, but they are not the same permission. An <strong>ETA</strong> is generally
      advance permission to travel under a visa-exemption programme. An <strong>eVisa</strong> is a visa whose application
      and issuance happen electronically.
    </p>

    <section class="term-comparison" aria-label="ETA and eVisa comparison">
      <article>
        <span class="status-pill status-pill--eta"><span class="status-pill__dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>ETA</span>
        <h2>Electronic travel authorisation</h2>
        <dl>
          <div><dt>Legal category</dt><dd>Usually visa-exempt travel</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Process</dt><dd>Short online pre-screening</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Result</dt><dd>Permission to travel, often linked to the passport</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Mobility score</dt><dd>Counts as accessible without a prior visa</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="status-pill status-pill--evisa"><span class="status-pill__dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>eVisa</span>
        <h2>Electronic visa</h2>
        <dl>
          <div><dt>Legal category</dt><dd>A visa</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Process</dt><dd>Online visa application and review</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Result</dt><dd>An electronic visa or downloadable visa document</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Mobility score</dt><dd>Does not count as access without a prior visa</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </article>
    </section>

    <h2>The practical distinction</h2>
    <p>
      An ETA is designed for travellers who do not normally need a visitor visa but must receive advance permission to
      board and travel. The UK describes its ETA as advance travel permission for specified non-visa nationals, while
      Canada describes its eTA as an online requirement for eligible travellers flying to or transiting through Canada.
    </p>
    <p>
      An eVisa changes the application channel, not the underlying document: it is still a visa. Azerbaijan’s official
      system, for example, reviews an online application and then issues or refuses an electronic visa before travel.
    </p>

    <div class="method-callout">
      <strong>Names are not perfectly standardised.</strong> Governments may use “ETA,” “ESTA,” “travel authorisation,”
      “eVisa,” or a local product name. Always follow the destination authority’s classification and current rules rather
      than relying on the label alone.
    </div>

    <h2>How MultiPass Rank treats them</h2>
    <p>
      The mobility score counts visa-free, visa-on-arrival, and ETA destinations. eVisas and traditional visas are shown
      in the detailed access table but do not add to the score because both require visa approval before departure. This
      is a deliberately simple mobility measure—not a judgment about cost, processing speed, or traveller experience.
    </p>

    <h2>What to verify before travel</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Whether your exact passport, travel purpose, route, and transit points are eligible.</li>
      <li>How long approval remains valid and whether it permits multiple trips.</li>
      <li>Whether the permission is linked digitally to your passport or must be printed.</li>
      <li>Whether a new passport, dual nationality, prior travel, or criminal history changes the process.</li>
      <li>Whether approval is only permission to travel; border officers usually retain the final entry decision.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Official examples</h2>
    <ul class="official-links">
      <li><a href="https://www.gov.uk/guidance/immigration-rules/immigration-rules-appendix-electronic-travel-authorisation" rel="noreferrer">United Kingdom ETA rules</a> — advance authorisation for specified non-visa nationals.</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/immigration-refugees-citizenship/services/visit-canada/eta.html" rel="noreferrer">Government of Canada eTA</a> — eligibility, application, and status guidance.</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.evisa.gov.az/en/conditions" rel="noreferrer">Azerbaijan ASAN eVisa conditions</a> — an official example of an electronically issued visa.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
